<h1>Likutay Halachos — Hilchos Tefillin, Halacha 5<br><span style="font-size:18px; color:#7a622e;">Part F: Sections 40–45 (Final)</span></h1>

<!-- =============== Section 40 =============== -->
<h3>§ 40</h3>

<p>Therefore Avraham <span class="bold-concept">purchased <span class="key-term">Me'aras HaMachpelah</span> from Efron</span>. <span class="key-term">Efron</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">afar</span> <span class="bracket-def">[earth/dust]</span> — the evil that grips the element of earth: "<span class="transliteration">V'nachash afar lachmo</span>" — "The serpent's food is dust" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 65:25)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">V'afar tochal</span>" <span class="source">(Bereishis 3:14)</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">atzvus</span> <span class="bracket-def">[melancholy]</span>, <span class="key-term">atzlus</span> <span class="bracket-def">[laziness]</span>, and heaviness — drawn from the evil within the element of earth, which is the essential bite of the serpent, as our Rebbe said regarding this verse <span class="source">(LM II:189)</span>.</p>

<p>Its subjugation comes through the holy element of earth — the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Sh'chon eretz ur'eih emunah</span>" — "Dwell in the land and feed on <span class="key-term">emunah</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 37:3)</span>. <span class="key-term">Afar</span> is "<span class="zohar-quote">ma'ana d'chulhu</span>" — the vessel that receives all. Through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> (= holy <span class="key-term">afar</span>), one receives and draws all <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> and holiness, and merits the <span class="bold-concept">power of growth and sprouting</span> — to grow and flourish in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span>, to pass over and leap over all obstacles without gazing at anything that would hinder. For <span class="key-term">emunah</span> = the power of growth = the power of sprouting, as explained thoroughly in the Torah of <span class="transliteration">Arichas Apayim</span> which we have been studying = the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore Avraham purchased <span class="key-term">Me'aras HaMachpelah</span> from <span class="key-term">Efron</span> specifically — for the <span class="key-term">kelipah</span> precedes the fruit. <span class="key-term">Me'aras HaMachpelah</span> (= <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span>, <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, growth-power) is the opposite of <span class="key-term">Efron</span> (= the serpent's contamination, melancholy, laziness — the side of death, the opposite of life). Initially it was captive in <span class="key-term">Efron's</span> domain — "<span class="transliteration">Es zeh l'umas zeh asah Elokim</span>" — "G-d made one opposite the other." Avraham, who is the head of the believers, merited to extract this exalted holiness from <span class="key-term">Efron</span>. Avraham merited holy <span class="key-term">ziknah</span> (= <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span>): "<span class="transliteration">V'Avraham zaken ba bayamim</span>" <span class="source">(Bereishis 24:1)</span>. He was the first to attain the holiness of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span> (= <span class="key-term">emunah</span>). Therefore his first acquisition in <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span> was <span class="key-term">Me'aras HaMachpelah</span> — for there one merits <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = <span class="key-term">emunah</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 41 =============== -->
<h3>§ 41</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">tisurah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[base/platform]</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, whose meaning is a <span class="bold-concept">bridge</span>. From it emerges the <span class="key-term">ma'abarta</span> <span class="bracket-def">[passage]</span>. This is the essential part that rests upon the body — for from the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, only the <span class="key-term">tisurah</span> and the <span class="key-term">ma'abarta</span> touch the skin of the head and arm. This is the essential thing we must receive from the holy <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: <span class="bold-concept">to draw from them a bridge to cross this world in peace</span> — to pass over all obstacles and confusions and draw truly close to Hashem.</p>

<p>The <span class="key-term">tisurah</span> = the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Yaakov</span>, who "<span class="transliteration">vaya'avor es ma'avar Yabok, vaya'aver es asher lo</span>" — "crossed the crossing of Yabok and brought across all that was his" <span class="source">(Bereishis 32:23-24)</span>. Rashi explains: "He made himself like a bridge." <span class="key-term">Ma'avar Yabok</span> = the <span class="key-term">ma'abarta</span>. Making himself a bridge = the <span class="key-term">tisurah</span>. Yaakov = <span class="key-term">Emes</span> <span class="bracket-def">[truth]</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Titen emes l'Yaakov</span>" <span class="source">(Michah 7:20)</span> = the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, as brought in our Rebbe's words <span class="source">(LM I:47)</span>. For the essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> come through <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emes</span>: "<span class="zohar-quote">Tzedek kad is'chabras b'emes is'avidas emunah</span>" — "Righteousness, when joined with truth, becomes <span class="key-term">emunah</span>" (as explained in LM I:7).</p>

<p>As our Rebbe brought in the Torah of <span class="transliteration">Arichas Apayim</span> — our Sages said <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 111b)</span> on the verse "<span class="transliteration">Vayikod artzah</span>" — "What did he see?" — "He saw <span class="key-term">Erech Apayim</span>" — another says: "He saw <span class="key-term">Emes</span>." And the two do not argue, for <span class="key-term">emes</span> and <span class="key-term">emunah</span> are considered one.</p>

<p><span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Emes</span> is the bridge.</span> As our Rebbe said <span class="source">(LM II:48)</span>: <span class="bold-concept">"A person must cross this world on a very narrow bridge, and the essential thing is not to be afraid at all."</span> To merit this — to cross the bridge of this world — is through the bridge of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emes</span>. As people say: "With truth, one can traverse the entire world." <span class="key-term">Emes</span> is the essential thing — the seal of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Heaven and earth were sealed with it: "<span class="transliteration">Bereishis bara Elokim</span>" — the final letters spell <span class="key-term">emes</span>. Upon this the whole world and its fullness stands.</p>

<p>All the confusions and obstacles in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> — essentially the obstacles of the mind — the essential counsel to overcome them is <span class="key-term">emes</span>, which guides a person to pass over everything: "<span class="transliteration">Shlach orecha va'amitecha, hemah yanchuni</span>" — "Send Your light and Your truth; they shall guide me" <span class="source">(Tehillim 43:3)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Ahaleich ba'amitecha</span>" — "I shall walk in Your truth" <span class="source">(Tehillim 86:11)</span>. <span class="key-term">Emes</span> is the holy bridge — as Yaakov (= <span class="key-term">emes</span>) made himself a bridge = the <span class="key-term">tisurah</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — by whose power one can cross this world all the days of one's life on the narrow bridge, without fear.</p>

<p>This is the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Kri'as Yam Suf</span></span> — when Hashem turned sea into dry land, froze the depths in the heart of the sea, and made a wondrous bridge for them to cross. All this was through Yaakov (= <span class="key-term">emes</span>): "<span class="transliteration">Mah lecha hayam ki sanus... milifnei Eloka Yaakov</span>" — "What ails you, sea, that you flee? ...Before the G-d of Yaakov" <span class="source">(Tehillim 114:5,7)</span>. Through <span class="key-term">emes</span> one can cross even through the midst of the sea: "<span class="transliteration">Ko amar Bor'acha Yaakov... al tira, ki sa'avor bamayim itcha ani</span>" — "So says your Creator, Yaakov... fear not, for when you pass through the waters, I am with you" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 43:1-2)</span>. "<span class="key-term">Bor'acha Yaakov</span>" specifically — <span class="key-term">emes</span>, through which: "Fear not, when you pass through waters..."</p>

<p>This is why on the <span class="bold-concept">third day of Creation</span>, Hashem said: "<span class="transliteration">Yikavu hamayim... v'sera'eh hayabashah</span>" — "Let the waters gather... and let the dry land appear" <span class="source">(Bereishis 1:9)</span>. The third day = Yaakov = <span class="key-term">emes</span> = the bridge through which one passes through all raging waters and is saved. The dry land in the midst of the sea = the bridge in the totality of the world, made on the "third day" specifically = Yaakov, <span class="key-term">emes</span> = the <span class="key-term">tisurah</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 42 =============== -->
<h3>§ 42</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">three wrappings on the middle finger</span>, which is on the level of <span class="bold-concept">Yaakov and Moshe</span> — the <span class="key-term">bariach hatichon</span> <span class="bracket-def">[middle bolt]</span> running through the beams from end to end (as explained in the <span class="key-term">Yanuka</span>, Parshas Balak). Therefore the binding of the <span class="key-term">Tefillin's</span> strap concludes there — for then <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is completed in perfection through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore we recite then: "<span class="transliteration">V'eirastich li b'emunah, v'yada'at es Hashem</span>" — "I will betroth you to Me with <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, and you shall know Hashem" <span class="source">(Hoshea 2:22)</span>. <span class="key-term">Emunah</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> depend on each other — but the essence is <span class="key-term">emunah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">V'eirastich li b'emunah</span>" first, and then "<span class="transliteration">v'yada'at es Hashem.</span>" It is bound on the <span class="bold-concept">middle finger</span> specifically — Yaakov = <span class="key-term">emes</span>, for <span class="key-term">emunah</span> depends on <span class="key-term">emes</span>.</p>

<p>"<span class="transliteration">V'eirastich li l'olam</span>" — "I will betroth you to Me forever" — <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span>, eternal life. "<span class="transliteration">V'eirastich li b'tzedek uv'mishpat uv'chesed uv'rachamim</span>" — these are the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Ateres Tiferes</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[crown of glory]</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> and four <span class="key-term">batim</span>: <span class="key-term">tzedek</span>, <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, <span class="key-term">chesed</span>, <span class="key-term">rachamim</span>. As Rashi explains: HaKadosh Baruch Hu makes a crown from these four upon the head of Yisrael = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, the essential crown of Yisrael. "<span class="transliteration">V'eirastich li b'emunah</span>" — the essential ultimate perfection is <span class="key-term">emunah</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 43 =============== -->
<h3>§ 43</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">knot shaped like a <span class="key-term">Yud</span></span> at the <span class="key-term">shel Yad</span> — the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">supporting the fallen</span>. They receive their <span class="key-term">Moach</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> — to revive themselves and not fall completely — through <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span>, as explained above. The <span class="key-term">Yud</span>-knot = the illumination of <span class="key-term">Tzaddik Yesod Olam</span> to <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> (= <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span>). Therefore one must be very careful that this knot sits close to the <span class="key-term">shel Yad</span> — for the essential illumination of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> for the common people (revived by the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span> from their fall) comes through the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik Yesod Olam</span> whose perception is so exalted that he can revive and support all the fallen.</p>

<p>The essential illumination of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is received most during <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">tefillah</span></span> — for then one merits complete <span class="key-term">Gadlus Mochin</span>, as in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span>. The essential <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> (= <span class="key-term">devekus</span>) = <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> and <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span> — conversation between oneself and one's Maker. This is the essential <span class="key-term">devekus</span>, as our Rebbe said: "The essential <span class="key-term">devekus</span> is <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>." Therefore they are called <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — from the word <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Naftulei Elokim niftalti</span>" — "I have wrestled a G-dly wrestling" <span class="source">(Bereishis 30:8)</span>. Rashi explains: "Twisted, contorted — <span class="bold-concept">I was obstinate and beseeched many twistings and importunings before Hashem, until He agreed with me.</span>"</p>

<p>In this manner, one must greatly increase <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> and <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span> all the days of one's life — this is the essential thing. <span class="bold-concept">One must be a great stubborn one in this</span> — even when it seems that one's words accomplish nothing, that one is very far from one's own words, that for years one has been at this and still has not achieved anything. Nonetheless, one must be <span class="bold-concept">stubborn like a truly stubborn person who does the thing without any reason</span>. So too in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span>, as our Rebbe said <span class="source">(LM II:48)</span>: one must be a great stubborn one in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span>. In truth, great stubbornness is needed in every area of <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> — but the <span class="bold-concept">essential stubbornness is needed most in <span class="key-term">tefillah</span></span>, supplication, and above all in the conversations of <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span>.</p>

<p>As Rashi explained on "<span class="transliteration">Naftulei Elokim niftalti</span>" — "obstinate and contorted, I was stubborn and beseeched many importunings before Hashem." <span class="bold-concept">Understand these words well, if you wish to live eternal life.</span> It is impossible to explain everything in writing. Translate for yourself in your own language the meaning of "<span class="transliteration">Naftulei Elokim niftalti</span>" according to Rashi — "obstinate and contorted" — and then you will understand to what extent one must strengthen oneself in <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span> and conversation with one's Maker, even though it may be in an obstinate, twisted manner, many times without taste or scent. <span class="bold-concept">For "He did not scorn and did not detest the prayer of the afflicted"</span> — even though it deserves to be scorned. Hashem is full of mercy at all times.</p>

<p>There are <span class="bold-concept">three categories of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span></span> <span class="source">(Zohar, Balak 195a)</span>: <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Moshe</span>, <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Dovid</span>, <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Ani</span>. These correspond to three aspects of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Moshe</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> (= <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> = the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>). <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Dovid</span> = the knot of the <span class="key-term">shel Rosh</span> (shaped like a <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> — also containing four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span>) = <span class="key-term">Malchus Mashiach</span>. <span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Ani</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> (= <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> = <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span>, called "<span class="key-term">Aniyah</span>") — illuminating all Yisrael with <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, reviving each one from his fall.</p>

<p>The <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillah l'Ani</span> is the most precious of all</span>, as the Zohar HaKadosh elaborates there at great length, praising the prayer of the poor one: "Broken vessels of HaKadosh Baruch Hu — <span class="zohar-quote">he constantly quarrels with HaKadosh Baruch Hu, and HaKadosh Baruch Hu listens and hears his words. When he prays his prayer, all the windows of heaven are opened. All other prayers ascending on high are pushed aside by this poor, broken-hearted one. HaKadosh Baruch Hu says: Let all the prayers of the world be wrapped aside, and let this prayer come before Me. I do not want any court to judge between us. Let his complaints be before Me — he and I alone.</span>" HaKadosh Baruch Hu secures Himself alone with those complaints, with that prayer: "<span class="transliteration">V'lifnei Hashem yishpoch sicho</span>" — "Before Hashem he pours out his prayer" — "<span class="transliteration">Lifnei Hashem</span>" indeed. All the hosts of heaven ask one another: "With what is HaKadosh Baruch Hu occupied?" They say: "<span class="zohar-quote">He has secluded Himself in longing with His vessels.</span>" None of them know what becomes of this prayer of the poor one and all his complaints — for the poor one's desire is only to pour his tears in complaint before the holy King, and the desire of HaKadosh Baruch Hu is only to receive them.</p>

<p>All this was said even about a poor person who prays about physical needs and poverty. How much more so when a person begins <span class="bold-concept">to have mercy on himself</span>, senses his poverty in good deeds, argues with Hashem, quarrels with Him, protests that He does not draw him close, and pours his prayer and heart before Him until he is stirred to tears — how exceedingly precious this prayer is before Hashem! It is the most precious of all prayers.</p>

<p>For in truth, this is the essential poverty: "<span class="transliteration">Ein ani ela min hada'as</span>" — "There is no poor person except one who lacks <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>" <span class="source">(Nedarim 41a)</span>. Beyond this, everything is <span class="key-term">hevel</span> — our days are a passing shadow. There is no affliction like the affliction of the soul, no poverty like the poverty of Torah and good deeds. All the more so when one is a great debtor who owes Hashem many debts from sins and transgressions and has no way to pay except through <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> and supplication. When this person stands before Hashem like a pauper begging at doorsteps, with a broken heart, pleading for his <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Mah betza b'dami b'ridti el shachas</span>" — "What profit is in my blood when I descend to the pit?" <span class="source">(Tehillim 30:10)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Hatzilah meicherev nafshi</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 22:21)</span> — surely this prayer is more precious before Hashem than all the prayers in the world.</p>

<p>Study every word of the Zohar HaKadosh cited above, and you will understand how precious is the <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span> of pouring one's prayer before Hashem. Even though one has been praying and secluding for so long and it seems one has still accomplished nothing — <span class="bold-concept">each and every word is not lost</span>; all are counted, numbered, and stored in His treasuries. Moreover, it is impossible for a person in this body to know whether he has accomplished anything in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> or not. Even if one accomplished only a hairsbreadth, once in all the days of one's life — that too is more precious than all the life of this world. For even when a person moves the slightest amount closer to Hashem — even less than a hairsbreadth — through this, <span class="bold-concept">thousands upon thousands of <span class="key-term">parsa'os</span> are traversed in the upper worlds</span>, as our Rebbe related in the story of the Tzaddik who once fell into great melancholy.</p>

<p>Therefore one must strengthen oneself in this beyond measure. When one is strong enough in this manner of "<span class="transliteration">Naftulei Elokim niftalti</span>," then ultimately, certainly, one will merit to achieve one's request — to draw close to Hashem, to be equal with one's worthy brethren and the Tzaddikim. As the verse concludes: "<span class="transliteration">Gam yacholti</span>" — "<span class="bold-concept">I was also able!</span> He agreed with me" — because I was so stubborn, beseeching many times, even though it seemed many times that my words would no longer avail. Yet I was stubborn with boundless stubbornness, and ultimately: "<span class="transliteration">Gam yacholti</span>" — He agreed with me, to be equal to my brethren, to draw truly close to Hashem. Amen and amen.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 44 =============== -->
<h3>§ 44</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">yahrzeit</span></span> — when one fasts on the day of a parent's death, recites <span class="key-term">Kaddish</span>, and lights a candle. All this is to elevate the <span class="key-term">neshamah</span> of the deceased to the <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span> (= eternal life that the Tzaddikim merit in the World to Come). This is done each year on the day of death — for <span class="bold-concept">the day of death is the true beginning of one's life</span>, each person according to what he merited through his holy service, since this world is a passing shadow and its life is not truly life at all. The essential <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span> = renewing one's <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> at all times, especially each year (= <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>). Therefore one works for the <span class="key-term">tikun</span> of the departed each year on his death-day (= the beginning of his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> = his personal <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>).</p>

<p>The essential <span class="key-term">tikun</span> is through one's <span class="bold-concept">children</span> — for good children are a great healing for the parents, as our Rebbe said <span class="source">(Sefer HaMiddos, Banim 42)</span>. The essential <span class="key-term">avodah</span> of a person in this world is to reveal Hashem's G-dliness. When his time comes to depart, he must leave behind a blessed remnant — worthy Jewish offspring who will fill his place and make Hashem's greatness known in the world. Each <span class="key-term">neshamah</span> has a unique power to reveal some aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> in the world. "<span class="transliteration">V'ad dor vador emunaso</span>" — "From generation to generation is His faithfulness" <span class="source">(Tehillim 100:5)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Dor l'dor yeshabach ma'asecha</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 145:4)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Zeh zichri l'dor dor</span>" <span class="source">(Shemos 3:15)</span>. All this = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> through childbirth (= renewal of generations revealing Hashem's G-dliness in each generation) = "<span class="transliteration">Kadeish li chol bechor peter kol rechem.</span>"</p>

<p>Therefore one <span class="bold-concept">lights a candle</span> — to draw light for the <span class="key-term">neshamah</span>, that it may shine in the <span class="key-term">Or HaChayim</span> (= the eternal life): "<span class="transliteration">L'hishalech lifnei Elokim b'or hachayim</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 56:14)</span>. And <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Kaddish</span></span> — established in <span class="key-term">lashon Targum</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Aramaic]</span>, for <span class="key-term">Kaddish</span> is above all holiness, and it shatters all iron bars, as the Zohar says. "<span class="transliteration">Brich Hu l'eila min kol birchasa v'shirasa</span>" — therefore it is in <span class="key-term">lashon Targum</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Nogah</span>, the Tree of Knowledge, the <span class="key-term">medameh</span>. For the highest holiness (= <span class="key-term">Kaddish</span>) has the power to purify and refine the <span class="key-term">medameh</span> (= <span class="key-term">Targum</span>) — transforming <span class="key-term">Nogah</span> to holiness. This = the holiness of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> written on hide.</p>

<p>As the Tikunim say: <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> for the Tzaddikim... for the wicked they are bound to all their accusers. Meaning: <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are such great <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> that they avail everyone, even all the fallen — for they are drawn from so great a <span class="key-term">Moach</span> that they can reveal that <span class="bold-concept">Hashem is with them and there is hope for their future</span>. Amen and amen.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 45 =============== -->
<h3>§ 45</h3>

<p>The entirety of this discourse revolves around the foundation built upon the story of the <span class="bold-concept">first Beggar of the Seven Beggars — the Blind one</span> — who gave the couple a gift as a <span class="key-term">d'rashah</span>: <span class="bold-concept">long life</span>. As our Rebbe himself explained with his holy mouth, after telling the story of the second day (about the Deaf Beggar) — he said afterward: "The first one gave them <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span>, and this..." For the essential aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is to merit <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span> — to begin living anew each time, true life in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span>, as explained above at length.</p>

<p>Therefore we say before donning <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> in the <span class="key-term">L'shem Yichud</span>: "And from the <span class="key-term">shefa</span> of the mitzvah of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, may long life be drawn upon me." For this is the essential aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — to merit <span class="key-term">arichas chayim</span>.</p>

<h3 style="color:#7a622e; border-color:#7a622e;">Addendum to § 45</h3>

<p class="no-indent"><span class="source">(Belongs to the above:)</span> Regarding the story in which each elder boasted of his memory — our Rebbe spoke at that time and said that something resembling this is found in the words of our Sages — that Shmuel remembered... But he did not explain it to us fully, and some of it was forgotten. Now I have found in <span class="bold-concept">Yerushalmi Kesubos, Perek "Af al Pi"</span>: <span class="zohar-quote">"Shmuel said: I remember the midwife who delivered me. Reish Lakish said: I remember the [mohel who performed my] circumcision. R' Yochanan said: I remember the women who wrapped [me] together with [my] mother."</span></p>

<p>Certainly these boasts of memory by these great, awesome Tzaddikim — the greatest of the <span class="key-term">Amora'im</span> — are wondrous insights. But all these memories are from after they entered the air of the world. They resemble the boast of the youngest elder (the first from below upward) who remembered the cutting of the navel. But what occurred <em>before</em> that — such as when the candle was burning above [the unborn child's head], etc. — and certainly what came even before that — no parallel has yet been found. And who knows which ancient Tzaddikim, fathers of the world, are alluded to there...</p>

<p>In truth, at the time he was telling this awesome story, our Rebbe said: <span class="bold-concept">"This story speaks of several ancient Tzaddikim..."</span> Set your heart to study the story well, and you will understand from afar the greatness of the Tzaddikim alluded to there, and the greatness of the one who told it. From all this, each person can understand — each according to what his heart perceives — the greatness of the Creator, blessed be His Name, who created the world with such deep wisdom that one born of woman can merit such awesome, mighty things: the memory spoken of here, true good life, the small containing the great, the power of the hands that can retrieve arrows after they have been shot, and so forth. Study it well and understand.</p>


